        "title": "Renaissance and Baroque Theater",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The Renaissance (roughly 1450–1600) and Baroque (1600–1750) periods produced the most theatrically diverse century and a half in history: the Elizabethan public theater in England, the Spanish Golden Age, Italian perspective scenery, and the invention of opera — all within 150 years. The printed text also transformed theater: Shakespeare's plays survived because of print; the theater itself remained oral and performative."
